From: Lumnus <lumnus.org@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 23:52:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 30/51] B70-IP-PAD16: TRITON_ATTN (K1/K2) follows the 16-B
 padded per-token-head slot

One definition of the per-token-head slot half in triton_attn
(pth_half_bytes / pth_half_ok / pth_check_half); customize_spec packs through
it and every reader validates against it or derives from the cache's content
width, never from 2 * (D + 4). INT8/FP8 halves are 16-B padded as in
vllm#62113 (hs 256: 520 -> 544 B, V at +272; hs 128: 264 -> 288); INT4 stays
tight. _ensure_scale_caches refuses a cache whose half cannot hold the impl's
head.

B70_PTH_PAD16 (default 1): 0 packs the halves tight again. Kept as an A/B
lever only (pool size vs vectorized loads on B70, and a bisect handle while
TRITON_ATTN int8 output on XPU is under investigation); all readers follow
either layout.

K1 TD guard stays on: the padded pitch is 16-B aligned, but the per-(block,
head) descriptor base is not 64-B aligned for V (+272 = 16 mod 64) nor, in
NHD, for K of odd heads (544 = 32 mod 64). Pointer loads now see 16-B
aligned rows and V base. Comment updated.

Tests (CPU, Triton interpreter): spec halves for hs 64/128/256 vs
vllm#62113; scale views on both layouts write nothing; a writer -> scale
views -> kernel_unified_attention round trip on both layouts, HND and NHD,
hs 128/256 (rel 2.5e-4 vs fp32 dequant reference; pad bytes never written).
